# DESCRIPTION

k1-emac drives an Ethernet MAC of the SpacemiT K1, as the Orange Pi RV2 has two of, each with a gigabit PHY on its own MDIO bus, and serves it as an Ethernet device, ether(3).
devmgr(8) starts one for each devicetree device that matches its record (driver(6)), after the APMU's k1-syscon(3):

```text
driver k1-emac
class net
match dt spacemit,k1-emac
dma-bits 32
```

It reads its node's properties at `/dev/dt/node` and its links at `/dev/dt/links` (devmgr(8)).
It first sets its pins' `default` state through k1-pinctrl(3)'s `ctl`, where the pin controller's driver runs, else leaves them as firmware set them and says so in `status`.
Then through the APMU's `ctl` at `/dev/provider/<service>` it turns the MAC's clock on, releases its reset, and sets its interface and delay lines (`emac`), as its `clocks`, `resets` and `spacemit,apmu` name them; without a running APMU it doesn't start the MAC.
`phy-mode` is `rgmii`, `rgmii-id`, `rgmii-rxid`, `rgmii-txid` or `rmii`; `tx-internal-delay-ps` and `rx-internal-delay-ps`, each 3962 at most, are the MAC's own delay lines, in steps of 15.6 ps.
The PHY is the one whose address `reg` gives under the node's `mdio-bus`, else the first that answers on the bus.
It resets the PHY, and on a Motorcomm YT8531 sets the RGMII delays the mode leaves to the PHY, 1.95 ns each, and turns its auto sleep off where the PHY's node has `motorcomm,auto-sleep-disabled`, as Linux's driver does; then has it advertise 10 and 100 Mb/s, half and full duplex, and 1 Gb/s full duplex, and negotiate.
It reads the link from the PHY's standard registers every second, sets the MAC's speed and duplex to it, and says so on the console: `k1-emac: <address> link up, <speed> Mb/s full duplex` or `link down`.
The address is the node's `local-mac-address` or `mac-address` (the loader copies firmware's into the tree), else the one firmware left in the MAC, else a random locally administered one.
It takes the frames sent to that address and broadcasts; flow control, checksum offload and VLANs are off.

Its receive and transmit rings each have 256 descriptors of 16 bytes with a 2 KiB buffer each, in memory the MAC reaches through its BTI: below 4 GiB, as its descriptors hold 32-bit addresses, and uncached where devmgr starts it with `--noncoherent`, as the K1's buses say.
A frame of more than 1514 bytes, without its FCS, is neither sent nor taken: one that arrives spanning buffers is dropped.
A thread serves the MAC's interrupt, and polls the rings every 50 ms against a lost one.
A frame written when every transmit descriptor is with the MAC is not sent.

# FILES

The driver's tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/k1-emac-dt-ethernet@<address>`.

: `ether`
  The device's ether(3) directory, which devmgr binds at `/net/etherN`.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `mac`, `phy` (its address and id), `link` (`up`, the speed in Mb/s and `full` or `half`, or `down`), a line each; then the counts: `received <n> transmitted <n> dropped <n> unsent <n>` and `interrupts <n> polls <n> causes <n> stops <n>`; then a line saying where the address came from, the PHY and whether the DMA is uncached.
  `dropped` counts frames received in error, spanning buffers or too short or long; `unsent`, frames with no transmit descriptor free; `stops`, the times a DMA said it stopped.
  If setup failed, `FAILED: status <n>`, with a line before it saying why where it can: no mode, no APMU, a command a provider refused, or no PHY.

`ether`'s `ifstats` reads as the counts' first line.

